+# Use SMS for pickup reminders
+
+For whoever changes pickup reminders or proposes email to save the SMS bill. Pickup reminders go by SMS because
+the email trial left more orders uncollected, wasting fresh food.
+
+## Context
+
+Volunteers have repeatedly proposed switching to email to save the reported SMS cost of 40 euros a month. Email
+was tried from April to June 2025. As recorded in [issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md), the provider reported that
+34% of reminder emails were never opened, and missed pickups rose from 6% of pantry orders to 13%.
+
+Many members share one phone in the family and do not check email; the pantry lead reports that they see SMS.
+Each missed pickup leaves a packed bag, whose fresh food goes in the bin at closing time. Reminder reach and
+avoiding missed pickups therefore matter alongside delivery cost.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email**: free to send, but the trial saw more missed pickups and wasted fresh food.
+- **SMS**: costs money, but reaches members who do not check email. Chosen.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep pickup reminders on SMS. The pantry switched back on July 1, 2025, and missed pickups were back to 6% by
+August. The maintainer reaffirmed this decision when closing issue 52. The channel is set in
+[`larder/reminders.py`](../../larder/reminders.py).
+
+## Consequences
+
+The pantry continues paying for SMS to reach members and reduce uncollected orders and food waste. Any proposal
+to switch channels should address the email trial's results and how it would reach members who do not check email;
+the saving on message delivery alone does not resolve those problems.
